WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.580
While Marvel films just seem to get bigger
and bigger with every passing year, Black

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.400
Panther continues to stand above its contemporaries.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.720
A record-breaking success, a pop culture phenomenon,
an Oscar nominee for Best Picture, and a film

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.680
that shattered barriers across the world,
this 2018 superhero movie was about as artfully

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.260
designed as comic book cinema could ever hope
to be.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.980
Black Panther packs in enough world-building
for an epic fantasy trilogy, introduces dozens

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.390
of memorable characters, adapts multiple comic
book story arcs, and sends the audience home

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.520
with a powerful, socially relevant message
that can't be forgotten.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.400
Before Black Panther was a finished film,
though, it was a script, a camera, and a whole

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.680
bunch of scenes that needed to be strung together.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.840
Editing is one of the most underappreciated
aspects of film production, in general, no

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.620
matter how good your material is, keeping
the pace tight means being ruthless about

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.480
which scenes matter and which don't.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.140
Hard choices are required.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:55.420
In a 2018 press interview, director Ryan Coogler
said that there was one scene in particular,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.560
set in the wake of T'Challa's apparent death,
that he was, quote, "incredibly proud of,"

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.490
but which he simply couldn't fit into the
film.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.900
Now, Coogler talks this scene up for a reason.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.780
Set in the Wakandan citadel, high above the
city, it depicts Okoye and W'Kabi in a tense

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.549
verbal confrontation over Killmonger's takeover
of Wakanda, which has resulted in T'Challa's

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:14.549
apparent death.

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:18.580
The two of them, both loyal to Wakanda above
all else, are split in regard to Killmonger's

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.560
goals.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.240
Aside from the phenomenal acting by both performers,
this scene also showcases the characters as

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.220
individuals, as leaders, and as two people
in a relationship.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.280
"This is what you would have me leave the
Dora for.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.880
To bring our children into this world where
they become conquerors."

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.040
"Yes."

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.120
In an interview with The Wrap, Black Panther
editor Michael Shawver, described this terrific

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.490
sequence as the one scene that was most heartbreaking
to cut from the film.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:44.440
He recalled,

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.520
"Toward the end, after things go bad and Killmonger
is in control and all that, we're talking

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.880
about, what are they going to do?

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.240
What is Wakanda going to become?...Those are
two powerhouse actors and it was an incredible

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.759
scene with so many layers to it, boyfriend
and girlfriend, it was general and her advisor,

00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.540
all those things.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:00.620
That was painful."

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.740
That said, both he and Coogler agreed that
it had to go.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.740
As Shawver put it, film editing requires being
hyper-aware of moments where the audience

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:08.860
might lose immersion.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.020
"At that point in the movie, it's about two
thirds through, and that's when most movies

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:13.520
drag.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.820
Ours was taking a while to get to that point."

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:20.480
The Okoye-W'Kabi scene wasn't the only great
moment to go splat on the cutting room floor.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.260
Shawver said,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.740
“There’s a scene between [T'Challa] and
[Zuri] which sets up their relationship.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.760
It sort of lets you attach yourself to their
father-son dynamic so that later on in the

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.120
film…you really feel more."

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.379
Coogler and company also made the tough decision
of cutting a great emotional beat between

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.200
T'Challa and his real father, T'Chaka.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.219
This scene is a flashback, set immediately
after the events in the opener, wherein T'Chaka

00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:44.760
kills Killmonger's father, N'Jobu, and it
shows T'Chaka back home in Wakanda, telling

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.500
a younger T'Challa of a difficult choice he
just made.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.820
"Some choices are harder than others."

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.460
"I am sure you did what is right for Wakanda.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.860
You always do.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.040
You are a great king, papa."

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.199
The young boy proclaims that when he grows
up and dons the Black Panther costume, he

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.160
will use his claws to ensure that his father
never dies.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.540
There's a lot of great foreshadowing here,
and the scene does help establish how the

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.640
roles of king and Black Panther aren't quite
synonymous.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.639
Still, you can see why it got cut.

00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:15.689
Nice or not, there's nothing critical here
that isn't established elsewhere in the film,

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:19.769
and deleting this scene allowed the narrative
to get back to the present day much more rapidly,

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:22.860
at a point in the story where pacing is particularly
crucial.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.720
Either way, every movie has to make cuts,
and Black Panther was no different.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.860
But Coogler and company can take pride in
having such high quality and emotional resonance

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.280
even in scenes that didn't make it to the
big screen.

